WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.197
By the end of the module, you should be able to normalize common text problems,

00:00:04.197 --> 00:00:11.362
choose the right temporal type, change time grain, calculate durations, and separate parsing from formatting.

00:00:11.362 --> 00:00:15.652
You should also be able to explain why a transformation is appropriate.

00:00:15.652 --> 00:00:18.972
The objective is not to memorize a long list of functions.

00:00:18.972 --> 00:00:23.997
It is to select the right function based on the meaning of the value and the analytical task.

00:00:23.997 --> 00:00:27.392
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example,

00:00:27.392 --> 00:00:31.957
an operations team aligning timestamps across locations.

00:00:31.957 --> 00:00:36.747
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:00:36.747 --> 00:00:40.017
analyst can understand our work.

00:00:40.017 --> 00:00:46.152
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to defend later:

00:00:46.152 --> 00:00:49.177
what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:00:49.177 --> 00:00:52.881
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
